                <pb facs="0686" n="591" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg591"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6156: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-05-01T14:00:00+01:00">May 1, 1936—2 p.m.</date><lb/>[Received
                        May 1—8:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>239. Foreign Office asked that Atherton call today with reference to my 186,
                    April 7, 6 p.m., and stated that the Treasury which is handling this matter was
                    unwilling to assume the responsibility involved by making any suggestions to
                    British bondholders in the settlement of this issue that had been negotiated
                    unofficially, although Leith-Ross had been kept informed of the course of the
                    negotiations. It was confidentially added that Leith-Ross had some 2 months ago
                    suggested that he might well return to England but the Chancellor of the
                    Exchequer felt on the contrary that his presence in the East continued to be
                    useful. In conclusion Foreign Office stated it was understood British and
                    American Bondholders Committees were in communication.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
